A||N||14||Warren Harrell||Pilot bushing problems||||8||
Z||000000||Warren Harrell||04-17-2001||09:22 PM||||Can anyone help me? I am having serious problems finding a new pilot bushing for my 2.8 liter fiero in any of the local parts stores. They keep giving me one that looks like it fits the 2.5. I know this because I used to own a Chevy S10 with a 2.5. If any anyone has any suggestions please reply.<P>  P.S. I also tried Using another car with a similar drivetrain ex. V6 Beretta. It did not help.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Archie||04-17-2001||09:25 PM||||The stick shift Fiero doesn't use a pilot bushing, that's why you can find one.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000002||Warren Harrell||04-17-2001||09:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>The stick shift Fiero doesn't use a pilot bushing, that's why you can find one.<P>Archie</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How is that possible? If I try to line up the clutch with the supplied lineup tool, the opening in the rear of the crankshaft is a quarter of an inch bigger than the lineup tool resulting in misalignment of the input shaft of the tranny<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||bubbajoexx||04-17-2001||09:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>The stick shift Fiero doesn't use a pilot bushing, that's why you can find one.<P>Archie</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>its about time they figure that one out||||reg||6
Z||000004||bubbajoexx||04-17-2001||09:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Warren Harrell:<BR><B> How is that possible? If I try to line up the clutch with the supplied lineup tool, the opening in the rear of the crankshaft is a quarter of an inch bigger than the lineup tool resulting in misalignment of the input shaft of the tranny</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>you line it up by eye the trans has two bearing one in the front and one in the rear hence no pilot bearing needed<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||04-17-2001||10:07 PM||||If anyone knows, it's these two guys. They're right, no bushing. look at the length of the shaft, I don't even think it's long enough to go into the crank. <P>I've never used a tool to line up the disc, just my eye.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||04-17-2001||10:54 PM||||Wrap a few rounds of masking tape around the end of your alignment tool to make it fit the crankshaft, then install clutch as usual.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||04-18-2001||01:54 AM||||YOU PEOPLE LINE IT UP BY EYE?<P>AM I UNDERSTANDING THIS CORRECTLY?<P>Are we talking about the plastic rod that fits to the end of the crank shaft that holds the clutch disk in place while the preasure plate is bolted and torqued down?<P>If this is so than there IS bushing that is made for it. We had like three of them (unmodified) at the shop. I didn't know they were scarce or non-existant!<P>Of course if this is not what you are talking about than I am in error and stand corrected!<BR>????????????????????||||reg||5
Z||000008||Carrolles||04-18-2001||08:15 AM||||Archie and Bubbajoexx are right.  The shaft isn't long enough to reach the pilot bearing if it is installed in the crank.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||04-18-2001||10:39 AM||||There is no pilot bearing in the crankshaft.<BR>You should never line a clutch up by eye. If your clutch assembly tool is too small, then you have the wrong tool.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mshill||04-18-2001||11:37 AM||||I am with Will on this one.  I have done two clutches in the last year and the only problem I had was forgetting to remove the bushing from the crank on my 3.4L before trying mount the clutch (oops).  My tool that came with my clutch fits perfectly.||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||04-18-2001||12:44 PM||||It does sound like we are talking about two different things!||||reg||5
Z||000012||bubbajoexx||04-18-2001||07:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>There is no pilot bearing in the crankshaft.<BR>You should never line a clutch up by eye. If your clutch assembly tool is too small, then you have the wrong tool.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>there is no manufacturer that makes a clutch alinment tool for front drive gm cars and the fiero is a front drive setup in the rear thyats why you line it up by eye once you press the clutch the disk centres itself.||||reg||8
Z||000013||EdsB52||04-18-2001||07:40 PM||||I have done a bunch of clutches on everything from jap-crap to fieros' to whatever and have never had to use anything but my eyesight and maybe an extension if I was in question. You slide the usually 3/8 extension into the clutch disc spline to see if you're close to centered between the pressure plate springs. When I have done 4cyl Fiero clutches' in the past, I have been able to procure pilot bushings for them. The tranny input shaft is kinda short, it may not have reached the bushing. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||thomas_l||04-18-2001||09:29 PM||||I just installed a new Luk clutch from Pep Boys with my junkyard 4 speed. There was a bushing in the box but it was for the 86 Cavalier I had them look it up as   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Also 2 alignment tools - one for the bushing and a fat one that worked with mine. My problem was that the correct kit had the wrong throw out bearing (I have an 87 GT). Oh yeah - there is no pilot bushing used on mine and the fat alignment tool worked perfectly.<p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 04-18-2001).]||||reg||1
